// Lesson 1.1: Simple Mechanisms — Levers, Linkages, and Mechanical Advantage
blockNum = 0;
const ch1_lesson1 = [
  t(1, "## What Is a Mechanism?\n\nA **mechanism** is a device that changes an input force or motion into a different output force or motion. Mechanisms make work easier, faster, or more convenient.\n\nAll complex machines — from a bicycle to a mining drill — are made up of simple mechanisms working together.\n\n### Input, Process, Output\n\nEvery mechanism can be described as a system with three parts:\n\n| Part | Description | Example (wheelbarrow) |\n|------|-------------|----------------------|\n| **Input** | The force or motion you apply | Pushing down on the handles |\n| **Process** | How the mechanism changes the force/motion | The lever (handles and wheel) |\n| **Output** | The useful result | The load is lifted and moved |\n\nIn South Africa, mechanisms are used everywhere — from the simple door handle at your school to the massive conveyor systems at Sasol's coal-to-liquids plants in Secunda."),
  t(2, "## Levers\n\nA **lever** is a rigid bar that rotates around a fixed point called the **fulcrum** (or pivot). Levers are the most basic type of mechanism.\n\n### Parts of a Lever\n\n- **Fulcrum (pivot):** The fixed point around which the lever turns\n- **Effort:** The force you apply to the lever\n- **Load:** The force you are trying to overcome (the weight being lifted or moved)\n\n### Three Classes of Levers\n\n| Class | Order of Parts | Example |\n|-------|---------------|--------|\n| **Class 1** | Effort — Fulcrum — Load | Seesaw, crowbar, scissors, pliers |\n| **Class 2** | Effort — Load — Fulcrum | Wheelbarrow, nutcracker, bottle opener |\n| **Class 3** | Load — Effort — Fulcrum | Fishing rod, tweezers, human forearm |\n\n### Key Rule\n\n- **Class 1 and 2** levers can multiply force (MA ≥ 1)\n- **Class 3** levers multiply speed and distance but require more effort (MA < 1)"),
  q(3, 'Which class of lever has the fulcrum between the effort and the load?',
    ['Class 1', 'Class 2', 'Class 3', 'Class 4'], 0,
    'A class 1 lever has the fulcrum between the effort and the load — like a seesaw. Class 2 has the load in the middle, and class 3 has the effort in the middle.'),
  t(4, "## Mechanical Advantage (MA)\n\n**Mechanical advantage** tells you how much a mechanism multiplies your effort force.\n\n$$\\text{MA} = \\frac{\\text{Load (output force)}}{\\text{Effort (input force)}}$$\n\n### What the Number Means\n\n| MA Value | Meaning |\n|----------|--------|\n| MA > 1 | The mechanism **multiplies force** — you need less effort than the load |\n| MA = 1 | No force multiplication — effort equals the load |\n| MA < 1 | The mechanism **multiplies speed/distance** — you need more effort but gain speed |\n\n### Example Calculation\n\nA crowbar is used to lift a 600 N rock. The effort applied is 150 N.\n\n$$\\text{MA} = \\frac{600}{150} = 4$$\n\nThis means the crowbar multiplies your force **4 times**.\n\n### The Trade-Off\n\nJust like in all machines — when you gain force, you lose distance. If a lever multiplies force by 4, the effort end must move **4 times** further than the load end. You cannot get something for nothing."),
  fb(5, 'Mechanical advantage is calculated by dividing the ___ by the ___. A class 2 lever has the ___ between the effort and the fulcrum.',
    ['load', 'effort', 'load'],
    'MA = Load ÷ Effort. In a class 2 lever, the load is in the middle — like a wheelbarrow where the wheel is the fulcrum, you push (effort) at the handles, and the load sits between them.'),
  t(6, "## Linkages\n\nA **linkage** is a system of rigid bars (links) connected by pivots (joints). Linkages are used to change the **direction**, **type**, or **size** of a movement.\n\n### Types of Linkages\n\n| Linkage | What it does | Example |\n|---------|-------------|--------|\n| **Reverse motion linkage** | Changes the direction of motion to the opposite direction | Windscreen wipers, scissors |\n| **Push-pull linkage** | Converts rotary motion to linear (back-and-forth) motion | Locomotive wheels to pistons |\n| **Parallel motion linkage** | Keeps two bars moving in the same direction and staying parallel | Desk lamp arms, toolbox trays |\n| **Bell crank linkage** | Changes the direction of motion by 90° | Bicycle brake levers |\n| **Crank and slider** | Converts rotary motion to reciprocating (back-and-forth) motion | Car engine (piston and crankshaft) |\n\n### South African Applications\n\n- **Windscreen wipers** on taxis and buses use a reverse motion linkage\n- **Mining headgear** at gold mines in Gauteng uses linkages to raise and lower the cage\n- **Bicycle brakes** use a bell crank linkage to convert the pull on the lever into a squeeze on the brake pads"),
  q(7, 'A bell crank linkage changes the direction of motion by:',
    ['90 degrees', '180 degrees', '45 degrees', '360 degrees'], 0,
    'A bell crank linkage converts motion through a right angle (90°). It is used in bicycle brakes where pulling the lever converts to a sideways squeeze of the brake pads.'),
  t(8, "## Cams and Cranks\n\n### The Cam\n\nA **cam** is a rotating piece with an irregular shape that pushes against a flat bar called a **follower**. As the cam rotates, the follower moves up and down.\n\n- The shape of the cam determines the pattern of movement\n- A **circular cam with an off-centre hole** (eccentric cam) creates a smooth up-and-down motion\n- A **pear-shaped cam** creates a sudden rise and slow fall\n- Used in car engines to open and close valves\n\n### The Crank\n\nA **crank** is a handle attached off-centre to a rotating shaft. It converts **rotary motion** (circular) into **reciprocating motion** (back-and-forth), or vice versa.\n\n- Turning a crank creates back-and-forth motion (e.g. old-fashioned sewing machines, hand drills)\n- A piston moving back-and-forth can turn a crank to create rotation (e.g. car engine)\n\n### Key Vocabulary\n\n| Term | Meaning |\n|------|--------|\n| **Rotary motion** | Movement in a circle |\n| **Reciprocating motion** | Back-and-forth movement in a straight line |\n| **Oscillating motion** | Swinging back-and-forth like a pendulum |\n| **Linear motion** | Movement in a straight line in one direction |"),
  fb(9, 'A cam converts ___ motion into reciprocating motion. A crank converts rotary motion into ___ motion (or vice versa).',
    ['rotary', 'reciprocating'],
    'Cams and cranks both convert between rotary and reciprocating motion. The cam uses its irregular shape to push a follower, while the crank uses an off-centre attachment.'),
  q(10, 'Which type of motion describes a pendulum swinging back and forth?',
    ['Oscillating motion', 'Rotary motion', 'Linear motion', 'Reciprocating motion'], 0,
    'Oscillating motion is a swinging back-and-forth movement around a central point, like a pendulum or a playground swing. Reciprocating motion is back-and-forth in a straight line.'),
];

// Lesson 1.2: Mechanical Systems — Gears and Gear Ratios
blockNum = 0;
const ch1_lesson2 = [
  t(1, "## Gears: Toothed Wheels\n\nA **gear** is a wheel with teeth around its edge. When two gears mesh (their teeth interlock), turning one gear causes the other to turn. Gears are used to:\n\n- **Change speed** — make something turn faster or slower\n- **Change direction** — reverse the direction of rotation\n- **Change force (torque)** — increase or decrease turning force\n\n### Driver and Driven Gears\n\n| Term | Meaning |\n|------|--------|\n| **Driver gear** | The gear connected to the input (motor, handle, pedal) — the one you turn |\n| **Driven gear** | The gear that receives motion from the driver — the one being turned |\n\n### Counter-Rotation\n\nWhen two spur gears mesh, they rotate in **opposite directions**. If the driver turns clockwise, the driven gear turns anticlockwise.\n\nIf you need both gears to turn in the **same direction**, you place an **idler gear** between them. The idler does not change the speed or force — it only reverses the direction."),
  t(2, "## Gear Ratios\n\nThe **gear ratio** tells you how the speed and force change between the driver and driven gears.\n\n$$\\text{Gear Ratio} = \\frac{\\text{Number of teeth on driven gear}}{\\text{Number of teeth on driver gear}}$$\n\n### Example 1: Speed Reduction\n\nDriver gear: 20 teeth. Driven gear: 60 teeth.\n\n$$\\text{Gear Ratio} = \\frac{60}{20} = 3:1$$\n\nThis means:\n- The driven gear turns **3 times slower** than the driver\n- The driven gear produces **3 times more torque** (turning force)\n- For every 3 turns of the driver, the driven gear turns once\n\n### Example 2: Speed Increase\n\nDriver gear: 40 teeth. Driven gear: 10 teeth.\n\n$$\\text{Gear Ratio} = \\frac{10}{40} = 1:4$$\n\nThis means:\n- The driven gear turns **4 times faster** than the driver\n- The driven gear has **less torque**\n- For every 1 turn of the driver, the driven gear turns 4 times\n\n### The Trade-Off (Again!)\n\n- **Large driven gear** (slow output) = more torque, less speed\n- **Small driven gear** (fast output) = less torque, more speed\n- You cannot gain both speed and force at the same time"),
  q(3, 'A driver gear has 15 teeth and a driven gear has 45 teeth. How many times does the driver turn for every one turn of the driven gear?',
    ['3 times', '1 time', '45 times', '15 times'], 0,
    'Gear ratio = 45 ÷ 15 = 3:1. This means the driver must turn 3 times for every 1 turn of the driven gear. The driven gear turns slower but with more torque.'),
  t(4, "## Velocity Ratio and Force Ratio\n\nGear systems involve two related ratios:\n\n### Velocity Ratio (Speed)\n\n$$\\text{Velocity Ratio} = \\frac{\\text{Teeth on driven gear}}{\\text{Teeth on driver gear}}$$\n\nA velocity ratio of 3:1 means the driver turns 3 times for every 1 turn of the driven gear.\n\n### Force Ratio (Torque)\n\nIf the velocity ratio is 3:1, the **force ratio** is also 3:1 — the output torque is 3 times greater than the input torque.\n\n### Worked Example\n\nA bicycle has a front chainring with 48 teeth and a rear sprocket with 16 teeth.\n\n$$\\text{Gear Ratio} = \\frac{16}{48} = 1:3$$\n\nThis means for every 1 turn of the pedals (front chainring), the rear wheel turns **3 times**. This is a high gear — good for speed on flat roads, but harder to pedal uphill.\n\nTo go uphill, you shift to a larger rear sprocket (e.g. 32 teeth):\n\n$$\\text{Gear Ratio} = \\frac{32}{48} = 2:3$$\n\nNow the wheel turns fewer times per pedal turn — less speed, but **more torque** for climbing."),
  fb(5, 'If a driver gear has 20 teeth and a driven gear has 80 teeth, the gear ratio is ___:1. The driven gear will turn ___ times slower than the driver.',
    ['4', '4'],
    'Gear ratio = 80 ÷ 20 = 4:1. The driven gear turns 4 times slower, but with 4 times more torque.'),
  t(6, "## Types of Gears\n\n| Gear Type | Description | Axis Change | Example |\n|-----------|------------|-------------|--------|\n| **Spur gears** | Straight teeth, parallel shafts | No | Clocks, hand drills, gearboxes |\n| **Bevel gears** | Cone-shaped teeth, shafts at an angle | Yes (usually 90°) | Hand drill (changes direction), differential in cars |\n| **Worm and worm wheel** | Screw-like gear (worm) meshes with a spur gear (worm wheel) | Yes (90°) | Guitar tuning pegs, fishing reels, crane hoists |\n| **Rack and pinion** | Small circular gear (pinion) meshes with a flat toothed bar (rack) | Rotary → linear | Car steering, automatic gates |\n\n### Worm Gears — Special Properties\n\n- Very large speed reduction in a single stage\n- **Self-locking:** the worm wheel cannot drive the worm backwards\n- This is a safety feature — the load cannot fall back once lifted\n- Used in lifting equipment and locking mechanisms\n\n### South African Examples\n\n- **Bicycle gears** — spur gears with chains transfer motion from pedals to rear wheel\n- **Minibus taxi steering** — rack and pinion converts the driver's turning into wheel movement\n- **Sugar cane crushers** in KwaZulu-Natal use heavy-duty spur gear systems"),
  q(7, 'Which type of gear converts rotary motion into linear (straight-line) motion?',
    ['Rack and pinion', 'Spur gears', 'Bevel gears', 'Worm gear'], 0,
    'A rack and pinion system has a circular gear (pinion) that meshes with a flat toothed bar (rack). As the pinion rotates, the rack moves in a straight line.'),
  t(8, "## Representing Gear Systems Graphically\n\nIn Technology, you must be able to represent gear systems using diagrams.\n\n### Gear Train Diagrams\n\nA **gear train** is a series of gears meshing together. When drawing gear trains:\n\n1. Draw circles to represent each gear\n2. Show the teeth meshing where gears touch\n3. Use arrows to show the direction of rotation\n4. Label the number of teeth on each gear\n5. Indicate which is the driver and which is the driven gear\n\n### Systems Diagram for a Gear System\n\n| Input | Process | Output |\n|-------|---------|--------|\n| Motor turns the driver gear at high speed | Gears mesh and transmit motion, changing speed and torque | Driven gear turns at a different speed |\n\n### Compound Gear Train\n\nA **compound gear train** uses more than two gears on separate shafts to achieve a larger total gear ratio.\n\nTotal gear ratio = Product of individual gear ratios.\n\n**Example:** Stage 1 has a 2:1 ratio. Stage 2 has a 3:1 ratio.\nTotal ratio = 2 × 3 = **6:1**\n\nThis means you can get a 6:1 speed reduction without needing an enormous driven gear."),
  fb(9, 'Two meshing spur gears rotate in ___ directions. A compound gear train achieves a total ratio by ___ the individual stage ratios together.',
    ['opposite', 'multiplying'],
    'Meshing spur gears always counter-rotate. In a compound gear train, the overall ratio is the product of each stage — e.g. 2:1 × 3:1 = 6:1.'),
  q(10, 'A worm gear is described as self-locking. What does this mean?',
    ['The worm wheel cannot turn the worm backwards, so the load stays in position', 'The gears are permanently glued together', 'The worm cannot turn at all', 'The gear ratio is always 1:1'], 0,
    'Self-locking means the driven gear (worm wheel) cannot drive the worm in reverse. This prevents loads from falling back, making worm gears safe for lifting applications.'),
];

