// Lesson 2.1: Types of Structures, Forces, and Structural Failure
blockNum = 0;
const ch2_lesson1 = [
  t(1, "## What Is a Structure?\n\nA **structure** is any object or system designed to support a load or withstand forces. Structures keep their shape under force and perform a specific function.\n\n### Types of Structures\n\n| Type | Description | Examples |\n|------|------------|----------|\n| **Frame structure** | Made of separate parts (members) joined together, with open spaces between | Eskom pylons, roof trusses, scaffolding, cranes |\n| **Shell structure** | A hollow structure with a thin outer skin that carries the load | Egg, cool-drink can, car body, aeroplane fuselage |\n| **Solid structure** | A heavy, solid mass of material | Dam wall, brick wall, concrete column |\n| **Combined structure** | Uses more than one type | A house (solid brick walls + frame roof truss) |\n\n### South African Structural Landmarks\n\n- **Frame:** Eskom high-voltage pylons across the country, mine headgear in Johannesburg\n- **Shell:** Soccer City stadium (calabash shape) in Johannesburg\n- **Solid:** Gariep Dam wall on the Orange River\n- **Combined:** Cape Town Stadium (shell roof on a frame skeleton)"),
  t(2, "## Forces Acting on Structures\n\nEvery structure must resist the forces acting upon it. Understanding forces is essential for designing strong, safe structures.\n\n### Types of Forces\n\n| Force | Description | What Happens | Example |\n|-------|------------|-------------|--------|\n| **Compression** | A pushing force that squashes a material | Members get shorter / thicker | Columns supporting a roof |\n| **Tension** | A pulling force that stretches a material | Members get longer / thinner | Cables on a suspension bridge |\n| **Bending** | A combination of tension and compression | Top is compressed, bottom is stretched | A shelf loaded with books |\n| **Torsion** | A twisting force | Material twists around its axis | Wringing a wet towel |\n| **Shear** | Two equal forces acting in opposite directions | Material is cut or torn | Scissors cutting paper |\n\n### Dead Loads vs Live Loads\n\n| Load Type | Description | Example |\n|-----------|------------|--------|\n| **Dead load (static)** | Permanent weight of the structure itself | Weight of walls, floors, roof |\n| **Live load (dynamic)** | Changing forces applied to the structure | People walking, wind, vehicles, furniture |"),
  q(3, 'Which type of structure is a cool-drink can?',
    ['Shell structure', 'Frame structure', 'Solid structure', 'Combined structure'], 0,
    'A cool-drink can is a shell structure — it is hollow with a thin outer skin that carries the load. The aluminium shell gives it shape and strength despite being very thin.'),
  t(4, "## Structural Members\n\nIn a **frame structure**, the individual parts are called **structural members**. Each member can be under tension or compression.\n\n### Members Under Tension and Compression\n\n- A **strut** is a member under **compression** (being pushed/squashed)\n- A **tie** is a member under **tension** (being pulled/stretched)\n\nIn a simple roof truss:\n- The **rafters** (sloping members) are under compression from the weight of the roof\n- The **tie beam** (bottom horizontal member) is under tension, pulling the feet of the rafters together\n- The **king post** (vertical centre member) is under tension, holding the peak of the roof\n\n### Types of Structural Members\n\n| Member | Shape | Properties | Common Use |\n|--------|-------|-----------|------------|\n| **Beam** | Horizontal bar | Resists bending | Floors, bridges |\n| **Column** | Vertical bar | Resists compression | Supporting roofs, storeys |\n| **Strut** | Any direction | Resists compression | Roof trusses, bridges |\n| **Tie** | Any direction | Resists tension | Roof trusses, suspension cables |"),
  fb(5, 'A member under compression is called a ___. A member under tension is called a ___. In a roof truss, the tie beam at the bottom is under ___.',
    ['strut', 'tie', 'tension'],
    'Struts resist compression (being pushed). Ties resist tension (being pulled). The tie beam stops the rafters from pushing the walls apart — it is pulled from both ends, so it is under tension.'),
  t(6, "## Strengthening Structures — Triangulation\n\nThe **triangle** is the strongest shape in structural engineering because it cannot change shape without breaking one of its members.\n\n### Why Triangles Are Strong\n\n- A **rectangle** can be pushed into a parallelogram (it deforms easily)\n- A **triangle** is rigid — its angles are fixed by the lengths of its sides\n- Adding a **diagonal brace** to a rectangle creates two triangles, making it rigid\n\n### Triangulation in Practice\n\n| Application | How Triangulation Is Used |\n|------------|-------------------------|\n| **Roof trusses** | Triangular frames support the roof sheeting |\n| **Eskom pylons** | Lattice structures made entirely of triangles |\n| **Bridges** | Truss bridges use triangulated frames (e.g. the old railway bridges in the Free State) |\n| **Scaffolding** | Diagonal braces create triangles to prevent collapse |\n| **Cranes** | The boom is a triangulated beam for maximum strength with minimum weight |\n\n### Cross-Bracing\n\n**Cross-bracing** is when diagonal members are added to a rectangular frame to prevent it from collapsing sideways. Each diagonal creates triangles within the frame. This is used in:\n- Building construction (temporary bracing during construction)\n- Furniture (diagonal braces under tables)\n- Shelving units"),
  q(7, 'Why is a triangle the strongest shape for use in structures?',
    ['It cannot change shape without breaking one of its members', 'It has the fewest sides', 'It is the easiest to build', 'It uses the most material'], 0,
    'A triangle is rigid — its shape is completely determined by the length of its three sides. A rectangle, by contrast, can easily deform into a parallelogram.'),
  t(8, "## Structural Failure\n\nStructures fail when they can no longer resist the forces acting on them. There are three main ways structures fail:\n\n### 1. Fracture\n\n**Fracture** is when a structural member **breaks** due to a force greater than its strength.\n- Caused by overloading (too much weight)\n- Caused by material fatigue (repeated loading and unloading over time)\n- Example: A wooden shelf snapping under too many heavy books\n\n### 2. Bending (Flexing/Buckling)\n\n**Bending** is when a member deforms without breaking, losing its original shape.\n- **Flexing:** Bending under load and returning when the load is removed\n- **Buckling:** Permanent bending, usually in thin members under compression\n- Example: A thin metal ruler bending when you push on both ends\n\n### 3. Toppling Over\n\n**Toppling** occurs when a structure becomes unstable and falls over.\n- Caused by the **centre of gravity** being outside the **base of support**\n- Prevented by making the base wider and heavier, and keeping the centre of gravity low\n- Example: A tall, narrow bookshelf tipping over if not anchored to the wall\n\n### Preventing Structural Failure\n\n- Use stronger materials\n- Add triangulation / cross-bracing\n- Widen the base to prevent toppling\n- Distribute loads evenly\n- Use the correct cross-section for beams (I-beam, T-section)"),
  fb(9, 'The three main types of structural failure are fracture, bending, and ___. A structure topples when its centre of gravity falls outside its ___.',
    ['toppling', 'base'],
    'Fracture = breaking. Bending = deforming. Toppling = falling over. A structure topples when the vertical line through its centre of gravity falls outside the base of support.'),
  q(10, 'Which of the following would help prevent a tall structure from toppling over?',
    ['Making the base wider and heavier', 'Making the base narrower', 'Raising the centre of gravity higher', 'Removing cross-bracing'], 0,
    'A wider, heavier base keeps the centre of gravity within the base of support, making the structure more stable. A narrow base and high centre of gravity make toppling more likely.'),
];

// Lesson 2.2: Frame Structures — Design and Evaluation
blockNum = 0;
const ch2_lesson2 = [
  t(1, "## Frame Structures in Detail\n\nA **frame structure** is built from a skeleton of separate parts (members) connected at joints. The spaces between the members are open or filled with non-structural materials (like glass or cladding).\n\n### Advantages of Frame Structures\n\n| Advantage | Explanation |\n|-----------|------------|\n| **Lightweight** | Uses less material than solid structures |\n| **Strong** | Can support large loads when triangulated |\n| **Efficient** | Material is placed where forces are greatest |\n| **Versatile** | Can be shaped for many purposes |\n| **Easy to maintain** | Individual members can be replaced without rebuilding |\n\n### Disadvantages of Frame Structures\n\n| Disadvantage | Explanation |\n|-------------|------------|\n| **Joints are weak points** | Connections between members can fail |\n| **Require bracing** | Must be triangulated to resist twisting |\n| **Can corrode** | Steel frames must be protected from rust |\n\n### Common Frame Structures in South Africa\n\n- **Roof trusses** — used on virtually every house in South Africa\n- **Eskom pylons** — carry high-voltage power lines across the country\n- **Mine headgear** — the tall tower structures above mine shafts in Gauteng and the North West\n- **Scaffolding** — temporary frames used during construction\n- **Steel-frame buildings** — used for factories, warehouses, and modern office blocks"),
  t(2, "## Designing a Frame Structure: The IDMEC Design Process\n\nWhen designing any structure in Technology, you follow the **IDMEC** design process.\n\n### I — Investigate\n\n1. Read the **scenario** or **problem statement** carefully\n2. Identify **who** needs the structure, **what** it must do, and **where** it will be used\n3. Research existing structures that solve a similar problem\n4. Analyse existing products for: fitness for purpose, materials, safety, cost, aesthetics, ergonomics\n\n### D — Design\n\n1. Write a **design brief** — a short statement of what you will design and make\n2. List **specifications** (requirements the structure must meet)\n3. List **constraints** (limitations: budget, materials, time, size)\n4. Draw at least **two possible design ideas** as freehand 3D sketches\n5. Label sketches with materials, dimensions, and features\n6. Evaluate ideas against specifications and choose the best one\n\n### Key Vocabulary\n\n| Term | Meaning |\n|------|--------|\n| **Design brief** | A short statement describing what you will design and make |\n| **Specifications** | Requirements the solution must meet |\n| **Constraints** | Limitations (budget, time, materials, size) |\n| **Evaluation matrix** | A table that scores each idea against the specifications |"),
  q(3, 'What is the difference between specifications and constraints in a design brief?',
    ['Specifications are requirements to meet; constraints are limitations to work within', 'They mean the same thing', 'Specifications are for materials only; constraints are for size only', 'Specifications come after making; constraints come before'], 0,
    'Specifications are the must-have requirements (e.g. must support 5 kg). Constraints are the limitations you must work within (e.g. budget of R50, must be completed in 3 weeks, maximum size 30 cm).'),
  t(4, "## IDMEC Continued: Make, Evaluate, Communicate\n\n### M — Make\n\n1. Create a **flow chart** showing the steps of construction in order\n2. List all **tools and materials** needed\n3. Follow **safe working practices** (safety goggles, clamps, correct tool use)\n4. Build the structure neatly, following your working drawings\n5. Use triangulation and cross-bracing where needed for strength\n\n### E — Evaluate\n\n1. Test the structure against the original specifications\n2. Check: Is it strong enough? Is it stable? Is it the right size? Does it look good?\n3. Identify weaknesses and suggest improvements\n4. Complete an **evaluation instrument** (checklist or rubric)\n\n### C — Communicate\n\n1. Present your structure to the class or panel\n2. Show your sketches, working drawings, and the physical model\n3. Explain your design decisions and how you solved problems\n4. Present a budget breakdown\n\n### Safe Working Practices\n\n| Rule | Reason |\n|------|--------|\n| Wear safety goggles | Protects eyes from flying material |\n| Clamp work securely | Prevents material from slipping while cutting |\n| Use tools correctly | Wrong use can cause injury and damage |\n| Keep workspace tidy | Prevents tripping and losing tools |\n| Cut away from yourself | Prevents cuts if the tool slips |"),
  fb(5, 'The five steps of the IDMEC design process are Investigate, Design, Make, ___, and ___.',
    ['Evaluate', 'Communicate'],
    'IDMEC: Investigate → Design → Make → Evaluate → Communicate. The process is iterative — you may go back to earlier steps if your evaluation shows problems.'),
  t(6, "## Working Drawings and Graphic Communication\n\n### Purpose of Graphics in Technology\n\nGraphic communication uses drawings to share design ideas clearly. In Technology, you use:\n\n- **Freehand sketches** — quick 3D drawings to explore ideas\n- **Working drawings** — accurate technical drawings with dimensions\n\n### Line Types in Technical Drawing\n\n| Line Type | Appearance | Purpose |\n|-----------|------------|--------|\n| **Continuous thick (dark)** | ━━━━━━ | Visible outlines and edges |\n| **Continuous thin (feint)** | ─────── | Dimension lines, projection lines, hatching |\n| **Dashed (hidden detail)** | - - - - - | Edges hidden behind the object |\n| **Chain line (centre)** | ─ · ─ · ─ | Centre lines, axes of symmetry |\n\n### Line Conventions\n\n- **Outlines** of the object are drawn with thick, dark lines\n- **Construction lines** (thin, feint) are used for layout and dimensioning\n- **Hidden detail** is shown with evenly spaced dashes\n- **Centre lines** use a long-dash-short-dash pattern\n\n### Dimensioning Rules\n\n- Use thin continuous lines with arrowheads\n- All dimensions are in **millimetres (mm)**\n- Dimensions are written above the dimension line\n- Keep dimensions outside the object where possible"),
  q(7, 'Which line type is used to show edges that are hidden behind an object?',
    ['Dashed line', 'Continuous thick line', 'Chain line', 'Continuous thin line'], 0,
    'Dashed lines (short, evenly spaced dashes) show edges and outlines that cannot be seen because they are behind other parts of the object. This is called hidden detail.'),
  t(8, "## Case Study: Electrical Pylons in South Africa\n\n### The Problem\n\nEskom must transmit electricity across thousands of kilometres of South African landscape — from power stations in Mpumalanga to cities like Cape Town and Durban. The pylons must be:\n- Tall enough to keep cables safely above ground\n- Strong enough to support heavy cables and resist wind\n- Lightweight enough to be transported and erected\n- Durable enough to last for decades in harsh weather\n\n### The Solution: Triangulated Steel Frame Structures\n\nEskom uses **lattice pylons** — frame structures made entirely from steel angles bolted together in a pattern of triangles.\n\n### Why Pylons Are Triangulated\n\n- Triangulation provides maximum strength with minimum material\n- The structure resists compression (weight of cables), tension (wind pulling), and torsion (uneven wind twisting)\n- The open frame allows wind to pass through, reducing the force on the pylon\n\n### Corrosion Protection\n\n- Pylons are **galvanised** (coated with zinc) to prevent rust\n- In coastal areas like Richards Bay, additional paint coatings are applied\n- Regular maintenance inspections check for corrosion and structural damage\n\n### Forces on a Pylon\n\n| Force | Source |\n|-------|--------|\n| **Compression** | Weight of the pylon and cables pushing down |\n| **Tension** | Wind and cable weight pulling on members |\n| **Torsion** | Uneven wind loads twisting the structure |\n| **Bending** | Cable weight creating bending in cross-arms |"),
  fb(9, 'Eskom pylons are ___ structures made from steel angles joined in triangles. They are coated with ___ (galvanised) to prevent corrosion.',
    ['frame', 'zinc'],
    'Eskom pylons are lattice frame structures using triangulation for strength. Galvanising (zinc coating) protects the steel from rust, especially important in South Africa\'s varied climate.'),
  q(10, 'Why do frame structures like pylons have open spaces between their members?',
    ['To allow wind to pass through, reducing force on the structure', 'To save money only', 'Because solid structures are illegal for pylons', 'To make them easier to see'], 0,
    'An open frame structure allows wind to pass through rather than pushing against a solid surface. This greatly reduces the wind force on the structure, making it safer and more stable.'),
];

