// CHAPTER 3: Processing and the Impact of Technology (Term 2)
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// Lesson 3.1: Processing Materials and Packaging
blockNum = 0;
const ch3_lesson1 = [
  t(1, "## Processing in Technology\n\n**Processing** means changing raw materials into useful products through a series of steps. Processing adds value to materials and makes them suitable for use.\n\n### Why We Process Materials\n\n| Reason | Example |\n|--------|--------|\n| **Improve properties** | Heat-treating steel to make it harder |\n| **Change shape** | Bending, cutting, or moulding material |\n| **Protect from deterioration** | Painting wood to prevent rot |\n| **Create a product** | Combining materials to make packaging |\n| **Add value** | Raw timber → furniture (worth more) |\n\n### Positive Impact of Technology on Materials\n\nMany natural materials have been replaced or improved in modern times:\n- **Plastic pipes** replaced lead and copper pipes (cheaper, lighter, no corrosion)\n- **Fibre cement** replaced asbestos sheeting (safer, no health risks)\n- **Bio-degradable bags** are replacing plastic bags (less environmental damage)\n- **Composite materials** like fibreglass combine the best properties of different materials\n\n### South African Context\n\nSouth Africa's manufacturing sector processes many raw materials:\n- **Sasol** processes coal into liquid fuels and chemicals in Secunda\n- **ArcelorMittal** processes iron ore into steel in Vanderbijlpark\n- **Sappi** processes timber into paper in Mpumalanga"),
  t(2, "## Investigating the Impact of Technology on the Environment\n\n### The Positive Impact\n\nTechnology has improved our lives in many ways:\n- New materials are **stronger, lighter, and cheaper**\n- **Environmentally friendly** alternatives are being developed (biodegradable packaging, solar panels)\n- **Recycling technology** allows materials to be reused\n- **Water purification** technology provides clean drinking water\n\n### The Negative Impact\n\nTechnology also causes environmental problems:\n\n| Problem | Description | SA Example |\n|---------|------------|------------|\n| **Air pollution** | Factories and vehicles release harmful gases | Sasol plant emissions in Mpumalanga |\n| **Water pollution** | Industrial waste and chemicals pollute rivers | Acid mine drainage in Gauteng |\n| **Land pollution** | Waste fills landfills, chemicals contaminate soil | Plastic waste in rivers and oceans |\n| **Deforestation** | Forests cut down for timber and farmland | Loss of indigenous forests in KZN |\n| **Resource depletion** | Non-renewable resources are used up | Coal mining in Mpumalanga |\n\n### Case Study: Plastic Shopping Bags\n\nSouth Africa introduced a **levy on plastic bags** in 2003 to reduce plastic waste. Shops must charge for plastic bags, encouraging consumers to bring reusable bags. Despite this, plastic pollution remains a major problem in South African rivers, beaches, and townships."),
  q(3, 'Which of the following is a negative impact of technology on the environment?',
    ['Air pollution from factory emissions', 'Clean drinking water from purification', 'Stronger building materials', 'Recycling of waste materials'], 0,
    'Air pollution from factory emissions is a negative environmental impact. The other options are positive impacts of technology on society and the environment.'),
  t(4, "## Packaging: Design and Materials\n\nPackaging protects products during storage, transport, and sale. Good packaging must fulfill several functions.\n\n### Functions of Packaging\n\n| Function | Description |\n|----------|------------|\n| **Protection** | Prevents damage during transport and storage |\n| **Preservation** | Keeps food fresh and free from contamination |\n| **Information** | Displays product name, ingredients, instructions |\n| **Marketing** | Attracts customers with colours, logos, and design |\n| **Containment** | Holds the product in a convenient shape and size |\n| **Convenience** | Makes the product easy to carry, open, and use |\n\n### Packaging Materials\n\n| Material | Properties | Products |\n|----------|-----------|----------|\n| **Cardboard** | Light, printable, recyclable, biodegradable | Cereal boxes, shoe boxes |\n| **Glass** | Transparent, reusable, does not react with food | Bottles, jars |\n| **Plastic** | Light, waterproof, mouldable, cheap | Bottles, bags, containers |\n| **Metal (tin/aluminium)** | Strong, airtight, long shelf life | Canned food, cool-drink cans |\n| **Paper** | Cheap, printable, recyclable | Labels, bags, wrapping |\n\n### Choosing Packaging Material\n\nThe choice depends on the product. Factors to consider:\n- Will it protect the product from damage?\n- Is it suitable for food (food-safe)?\n- Is it recyclable or biodegradable?\n- Is it cost-effective?\n- Does it look attractive to the customer?"),
  fb(5, 'The six main functions of packaging are protection, preservation, information, marketing, ___, and ___.',
    ['containment', 'convenience'],
    'Packaging must contain the product, protect it, preserve it, inform the consumer, market the product, and provide convenience (easy to carry, open, and use).'),
  t(6, "## Practical Activity: Designing Packaging\n\n### The Design Brief\n\nDesign and make packaging for a specific product (e.g. a container for 6 eggs, a box for a fragile glass ornament, or packaging for a school tuck-shop item).\n\n### Steps to Design Packaging\n\n1. **Investigate** — Look at existing packaging for similar products. What works? What doesn't?\n2. **Develop a net** — A **net** is the flat shape that folds up into a 3D container\n3. **Draw the net** accurately on card, using:\n   - Thick lines for cut edges\n   - Dashed lines for fold edges\n   - Include tabs for gluing\n4. **Add graphics** — Design a label with product name, logo, colours, and information\n5. **Cut, fold, and assemble** the packaging\n6. **Test** — Does it protect the product? Does it open and close easily?\n\n### Nets of Common 3D Shapes\n\n| 3D Shape | Net Shape |\n|----------|----------|\n| **Cube** | Cross shape — 6 connected squares |\n| **Rectangular prism** | Cross shape — 6 connected rectangles |\n| **Triangular prism** | 2 triangles + 3 rectangles |\n| **Cylinder** | 1 rectangle + 2 circles |\n| **Cone** | 1 sector (fan shape) + 1 circle |"),
  q(7, 'What is a "net" in packaging design?',
    ['A flat shape that folds up into a 3D container', 'A material used for wrapping food', 'A type of plastic bag', 'A digital design for printing'], 0,
    'A net is the flat 2D shape that, when cut out and folded along the fold lines, creates a 3D container. Nets are used to design boxes, cartons, and other packaging from card or cardboard.'),
  t(8, "## Waste Management and Recycling\n\n### The 3 Rs\n\n| Priority | Action | Description |\n|----------|--------|------------|\n| 1st | **Reduce** | Use less material in the first place |\n| 2nd | **Reuse** | Use items again instead of throwing away |\n| 3rd | **Recycle** | Process waste into new products |\n\n### Recycling in South Africa\n\nSouth Africa has a growing recycling industry:\n- **PETCO** recycles PET plastic bottles\n- **Collect-a-Can** recycles tin and aluminium cans\n- **Consol** recycles glass bottles and jars\n- **Mondi** and **Sappi** recycle paper and cardboard\n\n### Waste Pickers (Reclaimers)\n\nIn South Africa, thousands of **informal waste pickers** (reclaimers) collect recyclable materials from bins and landfills. They are an important part of the recycling chain, though they often work in difficult conditions. Efforts are being made to recognise and support their work.\n\n### Biodegradable vs Non-Biodegradable\n\n| Type | Breaks Down? | Time | Examples |\n|------|-------------|------|----------|\n| **Biodegradable** | Yes — broken down by bacteria/fungi | Days to months | Food waste, paper, cotton |\n| **Non-biodegradable** | No — stays in the environment | Hundreds of years | Plastic, glass, polystyrene |"),
  fb(9, 'The 3 Rs of waste management in order of priority are ___, Reuse, and ___. Materials that cannot break down naturally are called ___.',
    ['Reduce', 'Recycle', 'non-biodegradable'],
    'Reduce is the most important (use less). Then Reuse (use again). Then Recycle (process into new products). Non-biodegradable materials like plastic persist in the environment for centuries.'),
  q(10, 'Why are waste pickers (reclaimers) important in South Africa\'s recycling system?',
    ['They collect recyclable materials from bins and landfills, diverting waste from dumps', 'They manufacture new plastic', 'They design packaging only', 'They work in factories making products'], 0,
    'Waste pickers collect recyclable materials (plastic, cardboard, metal) from bins and landfills, sorting them for recycling companies. They play a vital role in South Africa\'s informal recycling economy.'),
];

// Lesson 3.2: Forces Acting on Materials and Material Properties
blockNum = 0;
const ch3_lesson2 = [
  t(1, "## Forces That Act on Materials\n\nWhen forces are applied to materials, the materials respond in different ways depending on their **properties**.\n\n### Reviewing the Five Forces\n\n| Force | Action | Effect on Material | Example |\n|-------|--------|-------------------|--------|\n| **Tension** | Pulls/stretches | Material gets longer, may snap | Pulling a rubber band |\n| **Compression** | Pushes/squashes | Material gets shorter, may crush | Standing on a cardboard box |\n| **Bending** | Pushes and pulls simultaneously | Top compressed, bottom stretched | Sitting on a wooden plank |\n| **Torsion** | Twists | Material twists, may shear | Wringing a cloth |\n| **Shear** | Opposite sliding forces | Material is cut or torn | Cutting with scissors |\n\n### How Materials Respond to Forces\n\n| Response | Description | Example |\n|----------|------------|--------|\n| **Elastic deformation** | Material bends under force but returns to its original shape | A spring stretching and returning |\n| **Plastic deformation** | Material bends permanently and does not return to its original shape | Bending a paperclip |\n| **Fracture** | Material breaks apart | Snapping a wooden ruler |"),
  t(2, "## Properties of Materials\n\nMaterial properties describe how a material behaves when forces are applied or when it is exposed to different conditions.\n\n### Mechanical Properties\n\n| Property | Definition | High Example | Low Example |\n|----------|-----------|-------------|------------|\n| **Strength** | Ability to withstand forces without breaking | Steel | Paper |\n| **Hardness** | Resistance to scratching or denting | Diamond | Chalk |\n| **Stiffness** | Resistance to bending | Steel beam | Rubber band |\n| **Flexibility** | Ability to bend without breaking | Copper wire | Glass |\n| **Elasticity** | Ability to return to original shape after bending | Rubber | Clay |\n| **Brittleness** | Tendency to break suddenly without bending first | Glass | Copper |\n| **Toughness** | Ability to absorb energy without breaking | Mild steel | Cast iron |\n| **Ductility** | Ability to be drawn into a wire | Copper | Concrete |\n| **Malleability** | Ability to be hammered into a sheet | Gold | Brick |\n\n### Other Properties\n\n| Property | Definition |\n|----------|------------|\n| **Conductivity** | Ability to conduct heat or electricity |\n| **Corrosion resistance** | Ability to resist chemical attack (rusting) |\n| **Density** | Mass per unit volume (how heavy for its size) |"),
  q(3, 'Which material property describes the ability to bend without breaking?',
    ['Flexibility', 'Brittleness', 'Hardness', 'Stiffness'], 0,
    'Flexibility is the ability to bend without breaking. Copper wire is flexible — it can be bent into many shapes without snapping. Glass, by contrast, is brittle and breaks rather than bending.'),
  t(4, "## Adapting Materials to Withstand Forces\n\nEngineers choose and modify materials to make them stronger and more suitable for their purpose.\n\n### Reinforcing Materials\n\n| Method | How It Works | Example |\n|--------|-------------|--------|\n| **Reinforced concrete** | Steel bars (rebar) are placed inside concrete. The concrete resists compression and the steel resists tension | Bridge decks, building foundations |\n| **Plywood** | Thin layers of wood are glued together with the grain alternating direction. This makes it strong in all directions | Furniture, construction formwork |\n| **Laminating** | Thin layers of material bonded together | Windscreen glass (glass + plastic film) |\n| **Corrugating** | Folding material into waves increases stiffness | Corrugated cardboard, corrugated iron roofing |\n\n### Choosing the Right Metal Section\n\nThe **cross-sectional shape** of a metal beam affects its strength:\n\n| Section | Shape | Strength |\n|---------|-------|----------|\n| **I-beam (H-beam)** | Like the letter I/H | Very strong for its weight — material at top and bottom where bending forces are greatest |\n| **T-section** | Like the letter T | Good strength |\n| **Angle section (L)** | Like the letter L | Strong in two directions |\n| **Channel (U/C)** | Like the letter U/C | Good for structural frames |\n| **Tube (round/square)** | Hollow cylinder or square | Strong, lightweight |"),
  fb(5, 'Concrete is strong in compression but weak in ___. Adding steel bars creates ___ concrete, which is strong in both compression and tension.',
    ['tension', 'reinforced'],
    'Plain concrete cracks under tension. Steel rebar provides the tensile strength, creating reinforced concrete that resists both compression and tension forces.'),
  t(6, "## Corrugation — Making Materials Stiffer\n\n**Corrugation** means shaping a material into a series of parallel ridges and grooves (waves). This dramatically increases the material's resistance to bending.\n\n### Why Corrugation Works\n\nA flat sheet of material bends easily. But when the same sheet is corrugated:\n- The ridges act like many small beams\n- The material is now much deeper (thicker in the direction of bending)\n- This increases **stiffness** without adding more material\n\n### Examples of Corrugation\n\n| Product | Material | Why Corrugated |\n|---------|---------|----------------|\n| **Corrugated iron roofing** | Galvanised steel | Flat sheets would sag between roof trusses; corrugation makes them stiff enough to span the gap |\n| **Corrugated cardboard** | Paper/card | Two flat sheets with a corrugated sheet between them create a very stiff, lightweight material for packaging |\n| **Concrete roof tiles** | Concrete | The corrugated profile adds stiffness to the thin tile |\n\n### South African Context\n\nCorrugated iron (\"sinki\") is the most common roofing material in South African townships and rural areas. It is:\n- **Affordable** — much cheaper than tiles or thatch\n- **Lightweight** — easy to transport and install\n- **Galvanised** — zinc coating prevents rust\n- **Corrugated** — the wave shape makes thin steel strong enough to span between roof trusses"),
  q(7, 'Why is corrugated cardboard much stiffer than flat cardboard of the same weight?',
    ['The corrugated (wavy) layer between two flat sheets increases depth and acts like many small beams', 'It uses more material', 'It is glued with a stronger adhesive', 'The flat sheets are thicker'], 0,
    'Corrugation increases the effective depth of the material. The wavy inner layer creates a series of small beams and air gaps, dramatically increasing stiffness without adding weight.'),
  t(8, "## Testing Materials — Fair Testing\n\nTo compare how materials respond to forces, we must conduct **fair tests** — experiments where only one variable changes at a time.\n\n### Steps for a Fair Material Test\n\n1. **Decide what you are testing** (e.g. which material bends least under the same load)\n2. **Choose materials** to compare (e.g. wood, cardboard, plastic, metal)\n3. **Keep everything the same except one variable:**\n   - Same size and shape of sample\n   - Same length and support method\n   - Same applied force\n   - Same testing method\n4. **Change only one variable** — the type of material\n5. **Measure the result** — how much does each material bend?\n6. **Record results** in a table\n7. **Repeat** to check for consistency\n\n### Example: Beam Bending Test\n\n| Material | Thickness | Length | Load | Deflection |\n|----------|-----------|--------|------|------------|\n| Cardboard | 3 mm | 200 mm | 100 g | 45 mm |\n| Plastic ruler | 3 mm | 200 mm | 100 g | 12 mm |\n| Wood (pine) | 3 mm | 200 mm | 100 g | 8 mm |\n| Steel strip | 3 mm | 200 mm | 100 g | 2 mm |\n\n**Conclusion:** Steel is the stiffest material (least deflection), followed by wood, plastic, and cardboard. The test is fair because size, length, and load were kept constant."),
  fb(9, 'For a material test to be fair, you must change only ___ variable at a time. Corrugation increases a material\'s resistance to ___ without adding weight.',
    ['one', 'bending'],
    'A fair test changes only one variable (the material type) while keeping all others constant. Corrugation increases stiffness (resistance to bending) by effectively increasing the depth of the material.'),
  q(10, 'Which metal cross-section is considered most efficient for resisting bending?',
    ['I-beam', 'Solid rectangle', 'Circle', 'Flat strip'], 0,
    'The I-beam is most efficient because material is concentrated at the top and bottom (flanges) where bending forces are greatest, with a thin web in the middle at the neutral axis.'),
];

