// CHAPTER 4: Mechanical Advantage and Gear Systems (Term 3)
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// Lesson 4.1: Calculating Mechanical Advantage and Gear Ratios
blockNum = 0;
const ch4_lesson1 = [
  t(1, "## Mechanical Advantage — Deeper Calculations\n\nIn Term 1 you learned the basics of mechanical advantage. Now we apply it to more complex calculations.\n\n### MA for Levers\n\nFor levers, mechanical advantage depends on the **distances** from the fulcrum:\n\n$$\\text{MA} = \\frac{\\text{Distance from effort to fulcrum}}{\\text{Distance from load to fulcrum}}$$\n\n### Worked Example 1\n\nA crowbar is 1.2 m long. The fulcrum is placed 0.2 m from the load end.\n\n- Distance from effort to fulcrum = 1.2 − 0.2 = **1.0 m**\n- Distance from load to fulcrum = **0.2 m**\n\n$$\\text{MA} = \\frac{1.0}{0.2} = 5$$\n\nThe crowbar multiplies force by **5 times**.\n\n### Worked Example 2\n\nUsing the crowbar from Example 1, what effort is needed to lift a 500 N rock?\n\n$$\\text{MA} = \\frac{\\text{Load}}{\\text{Effort}}$$\n\n$$5 = \\frac{500}{\\text{Effort}}$$\n\n$$\\text{Effort} = \\frac{500}{5} = 100 \\text{ N}$$\n\nYou only need **100 N** of effort to lift a 500 N rock — the lever does the rest."),
  t(2, "## Gear Ratio Calculations\n\nRecall the gear ratio formula:\n\n$$\\text{Gear Ratio} = \\frac{\\text{Teeth on driven gear}}{\\text{Teeth on driver gear}}$$\n\n### Speed Calculations\n\nIf you know the speed of the driver gear, you can calculate the speed of the driven gear:\n\n$$\\text{Speed of driven} = \\frac{\\text{Speed of driver}}{\\text{Gear Ratio}}$$\n\n### Worked Example 3\n\nA motor turns the driver gear (20 teeth) at 300 RPM. The driven gear has 60 teeth.\n\n$$\\text{Gear Ratio} = \\frac{60}{20} = 3$$\n\n$$\\text{Speed of driven} = \\frac{300}{3} = 100 \\text{ RPM}$$\n\nThe driven gear turns at **100 RPM** — three times slower, but with **three times more torque**.\n\n### Worked Example 4: Compound Gear Train\n\nStage 1: Driver = 10 teeth, Driven = 40 teeth → Ratio = 4:1\nStage 2: Driver = 15 teeth, Driven = 45 teeth → Ratio = 3:1\n\nTotal ratio = 4 × 3 = **12:1**\n\nIf the input speed is 1 200 RPM:\nOutput speed = 1 200 ÷ 12 = **100 RPM**"),
  q(3, 'A driver gear has 25 teeth and rotates at 200 RPM. The driven gear has 50 teeth. What is the speed of the driven gear?',
    ['100 RPM', '400 RPM', '200 RPM', '50 RPM'], 0,
    'Gear ratio = 50 ÷ 25 = 2. Speed of driven = 200 ÷ 2 = 100 RPM. The driven gear turns half as fast with twice the torque.'),
  t(4, "## Systems Diagrams for Gear Systems\n\nEvery gear system can be represented as a **systems diagram** with three parts.\n\n### Template\n\n| Input | Process | Output |\n|-------|---------|--------|\n| Force and motion applied to the driver gear | Gears mesh and transmit motion (changing speed, direction, and torque) | Modified force and motion at the driven gear |\n\n### Example: Bicycle Gear System\n\n| Input | Process | Output |\n|-------|---------|--------|\n| Rider pedals, turning the front chainring at medium speed | Chain transfers motion from large chainring to small rear sprocket (speed increase) | Rear wheel turns fast — bicycle moves quickly |\n\n### Example: Hand Drill\n\n| Input | Process | Output |\n|-------|---------|--------|\n| Turning the large handle gear slowly | Large gear meshes with small pinion gear (speed multiplication) | Drill bit spins very fast |\n\n### Drawing a Systems Diagram\n\n1. Draw three boxes labelled Input, Process, Output\n2. Connect them with arrows showing the direction of energy flow\n3. Write a clear description inside each box\n4. Include the type of motion (rotary, linear, reciprocating) at input and output"),
  fb(5, 'In a compound gear train with two stages having ratios of 5:1 and 4:1, the total gear ratio is ___:1. If the input speed is 1 000 RPM, the output speed is ___ RPM.',
    ['20', '50'],
    'Total ratio = 5 × 4 = 20:1. Output speed = 1 000 ÷ 20 = 50 RPM.'),
  t(6, "## Representing Gear Systems Graphically\n\n### Drawing Gear Train Diagrams\n\nWhen drawing gear systems, follow these conventions:\n\n1. **Use circles** to represent each gear (larger circle = more teeth)\n2. **Show meshing** where gears touch (teeth interlocking)\n3. **Use arrows** to show direction of rotation\n4. **Label** each gear with its number of teeth\n5. **Mark** driver (D) and driven (d) gears\n6. **Show the idler** gear if present\n\n### Direction of Rotation Rules\n\n| Configuration | Direction |\n|--------------|----------|\n| Two meshing gears | Opposite directions (counter-rotate) |\n| Three gears in a train (with idler) | First and last gear turn in the same direction |\n| Gear with chain/belt | Same direction |\n| Bevel gears | Axis changes by 90° |\n\n### Using Circular Templates\n\nIn the CAPS curriculum, you may be asked to use **circular templates** (compasses or circle stencils) to draw accurate gear system diagrams. Each gear is drawn as a circle with:\n- Teeth indicated on the circumference\n- Centre point marked\n- Shaft lines drawn through the centre\n- Rotation arrows clearly shown"),
  q(7, 'In a gear train with three spur gears in a row, the first and last gears rotate in:',
    ['The same direction', 'Opposite directions', 'Random directions', 'Neither rotates'], 0,
    'The middle gear (idler) reverses the direction once, then the third gear reverses it again. So the first and last gears turn in the same direction.'),
  t(8, "## PAT Scenario: Mining Head-Gear\n\nThe Practical Assessment Task (PAT) in Term 3 is based on a real-world scenario involving South African mining.\n\n### The Scenario\n\nSouth Africa is rich in mineral resources — gold, platinum, coal, manganese, chromium, and iron ore. A mining company needs a **lifting mechanism** (wire rope-driven mine head-gear) for a shaft.\n\n### What You Must Do\n\n1. **Investigate** — Research how mine head-gear works, what gear systems are used to raise and lower the cage\n2. **Design** — Create a design brief with specifications and constraints, draw sketches of your solution\n3. **Calculate** — Work out gear ratios, mechanical advantage, and speed/force relationships\n4. **Make** — Build a working scale model using gears to demonstrate the lifting mechanism\n5. **Evaluate** — Test your model and assess how well it meets the design brief\n\n### Key Concepts for the PAT\n\n| Concept | Application |\n|---------|------------|\n| Gear ratio | Calculate the speed reduction needed for safe lifting |\n| Mechanical advantage | How much force the gear system multiplies |\n| Torque | The turning force needed to lift the cage |\n| Direction of rotation | Ensuring the cage goes up and down as needed |\n| Safety | Self-locking worm gear to prevent the cage from falling |"),
  fb(9, 'South Africa is a major producer of gold, platinum, and ___. In the PAT scenario, a ___ gear is used for safety because it is self-locking.',
    ['coal', 'worm'],
    'South Africa has vast reserves of gold, platinum, coal, and many other minerals. A worm gear is self-locking — the worm wheel cannot drive the worm backwards — making it safe for lifting heavy loads.'),
  q(10, 'A lever has its fulcrum 0.3 m from the load and 0.9 m from the effort. What is the mechanical advantage?',
    ['3', '0.3', '1.2', '0.9'], 0,
    'MA = distance from effort to fulcrum ÷ distance from load to fulcrum = 0.9 ÷ 0.3 = 3. The lever multiplies force by 3.'),
];

// Lesson 4.2: Impact of Technology and Indigenous Knowledge Systems
blockNum = 0;
const ch4_lesson2 = [
  t(1, "## Impact of Technology on Society\n\nTechnology has both **positive** and **negative** impacts on society, the economy, and the environment. As technology students, you must be able to analyse these impacts critically.\n\n### Positive Impacts\n\n| Area | Positive Impact | Example |\n|------|----------------|--------|\n| **Employment** | Creates new jobs in manufacturing, IT, engineering | Automotive factories in Gqeberha (Port Elizabeth) employ thousands |\n| **Health** | Better medical equipment, cleaner water, vaccines | Water purification plants serving rural communities |\n| **Communication** | Connects people across distances instantly | Mobile phones connecting rural South Africa |\n| **Education** | Access to information and learning resources | Tablets and internet in South African schools |\n| **Transport** | Faster, safer travel for people and goods | Gautrain rapid rail system in Gauteng |\n\n### Negative Impacts\n\n| Area | Negative Impact | Example |\n|------|----------------|--------|\n| **Job losses** | Machines replace human workers | Automated mining equipment replacing manual labourers |\n| **Pollution** | Factory waste, vehicle emissions | Air pollution in the Vaal Triangle industrial area |\n| **Health risks** | Exposure to harmful chemicals and radiation | Asbestos-related diseases in former mining areas |\n| **Social inequality** | Technology gap between rich and poor | Digital divide — not all South Africans have internet access |\n| **Waste** | Electronic waste (e-waste) is difficult to dispose of | Old phones and computers in landfills |"),
  t(2, "## Bias in Technology\n\n**Bias** in technology means that technology does not always benefit everyone equally. It can favour certain groups while disadvantaging others.\n\n### Types of Bias\n\n| Bias | Description | Example |\n|------|------------|--------|\n| **Gender bias** | Technology jobs and products favour one gender | Engineering and mining have historically excluded women |\n| **Economic bias** | Only wealthy people can afford new technology | Expensive smartphones vs basic phones |\n| **Cultural bias** | Technology reflects one culture's values | Software designed in English only, ignoring African languages |\n| **Geographic bias** | Technology benefits urban areas more than rural | Fast internet in cities, poor connectivity in rural areas |\n| **Access bias** | Not everyone has equal access to technology | Schools in townships may lack computers and internet |\n\n### Addressing Bias in South Africa\n\n- Government programmes to bring **internet access** to rural schools\n- **STEM programmes** encouraging girls to study engineering, science, and technology\n- **Indigenous knowledge** being recognised alongside modern technology\n- **Universal Service and Access Agency of South Africa (USAASA)** works to bridge the digital divide"),
  q(3, 'What does "bias in technology" mean?',
    ['Technology does not benefit everyone equally — it can favour some groups over others', 'Technology is always unfair', 'All technology is biased and should be banned', 'Only old technology is biased'], 0,
    'Bias means technology favours some groups while disadvantaging others. This can be based on gender, economic status, culture, geography, or access. Identifying bias helps us create more equitable technology solutions.'),
  t(4, "## Indigenous Knowledge Systems (IKS)\n\nSouth Africa has rich **indigenous knowledge systems** — technologies and practices developed by local communities over centuries, long before modern Western technology.\n\n### Examples of Indigenous Technology in South Africa\n\n| IKS | Description | Modern Equivalent |\n|-----|------------|-------------------|\n| **Thatched roof (rondavel)** | Grass/reeds woven into a waterproof, insulated roof | Conventional roof tiles |\n| **Clay grain storage pots** | Underground or raised clay containers keeping grain dry and pest-free | Metal silos |\n| **Iron smelting** | Extracting iron from ore using clay furnaces and charcoal — done in SA for over 1 000 years | Modern blast furnaces |\n| **Herbal medicine** | Traditional healers using indigenous plants for treatment | Pharmaceutical drugs |\n| **Beehive huts** | Shell structures using woven branches and thatch | Modern dome structures |\n| **Fish traps** | Woven baskets placed in rivers to catch fish | Commercial fishing nets |\n\n### Iron Smelting in Pre-Colonial South Africa\n\nArchaeological evidence shows that communities in Limpopo, KwaZulu-Natal, and Mpumalanga were smelting iron as early as **200 AD** — more than 1 800 years ago. They used:\n- **Clay furnaces** built to reach temperatures above 1 200°C\n- **Charcoal** as fuel (made from burning wood with limited oxygen)\n- **Bellows** to pump air into the furnace, increasing the temperature\n- The iron produced was used for tools, weapons, and farming implements"),
  fb(5, 'Indigenous iron smelting in South Africa dates back to approximately ___ AD. Traditional grain storage used raised ___ pots to keep grain dry and free from pests.',
    ['200', 'clay'],
    'Iron smelting evidence in southern Africa dates to about 200 AD. Clay pots raised off the ground kept grain dry (preventing mould) and away from rats and insects.'),
  t(6, "## Career Choices and Opportunities in Technology\n\nTechnology education opens doors to many career paths in South Africa.\n\n### Careers Related to Technology\n\n| Career | Description | Where in SA |\n|--------|------------|------------|\n| **Mechanical engineer** | Designs machines and mechanical systems | Manufacturing plants, mines, Eskom |\n| **Civil engineer** | Designs and builds infrastructure (roads, bridges, buildings) | SANRAL, construction companies |\n| **Electrical engineer** | Designs electrical and electronic systems | Eskom, Telkom, electronics firms |\n| **Architect** | Designs buildings and structures | Private practices, government |\n| **Technician** | Installs, maintains, and repairs equipment | Factories, mines, telecommunications |\n| **Artisan** | Skilled trade worker (welder, electrician, plumber, fitter) | Construction, manufacturing |\n| **Industrial designer** | Designs products for manufacture | Consumer goods companies |\n| **Environmental scientist** | Studies and protects the environment | Government, NGOs, mines |\n\n### Addressing Gender Bias in STEM\n\n- Historically, engineering and technology careers were dominated by men\n- South Africa has programmes to encourage **women in STEM** (Science, Technology, Engineering, Mathematics)\n- The **Department of Science and Innovation** funds bursaries for women in engineering\n- Companies are required to promote **Employment Equity** in technical roles"),
  q(7, 'Which of the following is an example of indigenous technology in South Africa?',
    ['Iron smelting using clay furnaces and charcoal', 'Building with reinforced concrete', 'Using solar panels', 'Manufacturing plastic products'], 0,
    'Iron smelting using clay furnaces and charcoal is a technology developed by indigenous communities in southern Africa over 1 800 years ago. The other options are modern technologies.'),
  t(8, "## Investigating the Impact of Mining on the Environment\n\nMining is one of South Africa's most important industries, but it has significant environmental impacts.\n\n### Positive Impacts of Mining\n\n- **Employment** — the mining industry employs over 450 000 people in SA\n- **Economic growth** — minerals are a major source of export income\n- **Infrastructure** — mines build roads, schools, and clinics in remote areas\n- **Technological development** — mining drives innovation in engineering\n\n### Negative Environmental Impacts\n\n| Impact | Description |\n|--------|------------|\n| **Acid mine drainage (AMD)** | Acidic water from abandoned mines pollutes rivers and groundwater — a major problem in Gauteng |\n| **Dust pollution** | Mining creates fine dust particles that cause respiratory diseases |\n| **Land degradation** | Open-cast mining destroys the landscape, leaving massive scars |\n| **Water consumption** | Mining uses large volumes of water in a water-scarce country |\n| **Tailings dams** | Dams holding waste material can fail, causing floods of toxic material |\n\n### Balancing Development and Environment\n\n- The **Mineral and Petroleum Resources Development Act** requires mines to have **environmental management plans**\n- Mines must **rehabilitate** (restore) the land after mining is complete\n- **Environmental Impact Assessments (EIAs)** must be conducted before a new mine can open"),
  fb(9, 'Acid mine drainage (AMD) is a major environmental problem in ___ province. Before a new mine can open, an Environmental ___ Assessment (EIA) must be conducted.',
    ['Gauteng', 'Impact'],
    'AMD is particularly severe in Gauteng, where water from old gold mines rises to the surface carrying acids and heavy metals. EIAs are mandatory assessments of the environmental consequences of proposed developments.'),
  q(10, 'Why is it important to study both modern technology and indigenous knowledge systems?',
    ['IKS provides sustainable, locally appropriate solutions that can complement modern technology', 'IKS is always better than modern technology', 'Modern technology has completely replaced IKS', 'There is no connection between IKS and modern technology'], 0,
    'Indigenous knowledge offers time-tested, sustainable solutions developed for local conditions. Combining IKS with modern technology can create better, more culturally appropriate solutions — especially in developing countries like South Africa.'),
];

