// Lesson 5.1: Circuit Components, Symbols, and Simple Circuits
blockNum = 0;
const ch5_lesson1 = [
  t(1, "## Introduction to Electrical Systems\n\nAn **electrical circuit** is a closed pathway through which **electric current** flows. Current is the movement of tiny charged particles (electrons) through a conductor.\n\n### What You Need for a Circuit\n\nFor electric current to flow, you need:\n1. An **energy source** — a cell or battery to push the electrons\n2. A **conductor** — a wire or material that allows electrons to flow\n3. A **load** — a component that uses the energy (lamp, buzzer, motor)\n4. A **complete (closed) pathway** — no gaps in the circuit\n\nIf any part of the circuit is broken (open), current cannot flow.\n\n### Conductors and Insulators\n\n| Type | Description | Examples |\n|------|------------|----------|\n| **Conductor** | Allows electric current to flow through it | Copper, aluminium, steel, gold, water |\n| **Insulator** | Does not allow electric current to flow | Plastic, rubber, wood, glass, air |\n\n### Why Copper?\n\nCopper is the most common conductor for electrical wiring because it is:\n- An excellent conductor (low resistance)\n- Flexible (can be bent without breaking)\n- Relatively affordable\n- Does not corrode easily"),
  t(2, "## Circuit Components and Their Symbols\n\nIn Technology, you must know the standard symbols for electrical components.\n\n| Component | Symbol Description | Function |\n|-----------|-------------------|----------|\n| **Cell** | One long thin line (+) and one short thick line (−) | Provides a small voltage (typically 1.5 V) |\n| **Battery** | Two or more cells joined in series | Provides a larger voltage |\n| **Lamp (bulb)** | Circle with an X inside | Converts electrical energy to light and heat |\n| **Switch** | Gap with a moveable arm | Opens or closes the circuit |\n| **Resistor** | Rectangle | Opposes the flow of current |\n| **Variable resistor** | Rectangle with an arrow through it | Adjustable resistance |\n| **Motor** | Circle with M inside | Converts electrical energy to rotary motion |\n| **Buzzer** | Half-circle shape | Converts electrical energy to sound |\n| **Ammeter** | Circle with A inside | Measures current (connected in series) |\n| **Voltmeter** | Circle with V inside | Measures voltage (connected in parallel) |\n| **LED** | Diode symbol with two arrows pointing outward | Emits light when current flows through it |\n\n### Drawing Circuit Diagrams\n\n- Use a **ruler** for all straight lines (horizontal and vertical)\n- Use **correct symbols** for each component\n- Do not draw wires crossing unless they are actually connected\n- Label components with their values (e.g. R = 100 Ω)"),
  q(3, 'Which component is represented by a circle with the letter A inside?',
    ['Ammeter', 'Voltmeter', 'Motor', 'Battery'], 0,
    'An ammeter measures current strength and is shown as a circle with A inside. It must be connected in series so that all the current flows through it.'),
  t(4, "## Simple Circuit Components\n\n### The Cell and Battery\n\n- A **cell** provides about 1.5 V of electrical energy (e.g. an AA or D cell)\n- A **battery** is two or more cells connected together\n- Cells in **series** (end-to-end): voltages add up. Three 1.5 V cells = 4.5 V\n- Cells in **parallel** (side-by-side): voltage stays the same (1.5 V) but the battery lasts longer\n\n### Input, Process, and Output Devices\n\nElectrical components can be classified by their role in the circuit:\n\n| Category | Role | Examples |\n|----------|------|----------|\n| **Input** | Provides energy or information | Cell/battery, switch, sensor |\n| **Process/Control** | Controls the flow of current | Resistor, variable resistor |\n| **Output** | Converts electrical energy to another form | Lamp (light), buzzer (sound), motor (movement), heater (heat) |\n\n### The Switch\n\nA switch controls whether the circuit is **open** (off) or **closed** (on):\n- **Closed switch** — the circuit is complete, current flows\n- **Open switch** — there is a gap, current cannot flow\n\nSome devices act as both input and output. For example, a **solar panel** (input — converts light to electricity) and a **generator** (input — converts motion to electricity)."),
  fb(5, 'Three 1.5 V cells connected in series produce a total voltage of ___ V. An ammeter must be connected in ___ with the circuit to measure current.',
    ['4.5', 'series'],
    'Cells in series: voltages add up (1.5 + 1.5 + 1.5 = 4.5 V). An ammeter is connected in series so all the current flows through it and can be measured.'),
  t(6, "## Correct Connections in Circuits\n\n### Setting Up a Simple Circuit\n\nA basic circuit consists of: battery → switch → load (lamp) → back to battery.\n\n**Steps to set up:**\n1. Connect one wire from the positive terminal of the battery to one side of the switch\n2. Connect a wire from the other side of the switch to one terminal of the lamp\n3. Connect a wire from the other terminal of the lamp back to the negative terminal of the battery\n4. Close the switch — the lamp should light up\n\n### Short Circuits\n\nA **short circuit** occurs when current finds a shortcut back to the battery without passing through the load.\n\n- The wire has very low resistance, so a **very large current** flows\n- This can cause the wire to **overheat, melt, or catch fire**\n- The battery drains very quickly\n- **Never** connect a wire directly from the positive to the negative terminal\n\n### Safety Rules for Working with Circuits\n\n| Rule | Reason |\n|------|--------|\n| Only use low-voltage batteries (1.5 V to 9 V) | Mains electricity (230 V) can be fatal |\n| Never create short circuits | Causes overheating and fire risk |\n| Check all connections before switching on | Prevents damage to components |\n| Disconnect battery before changing the circuit | Prevents shocks and shorts |\n| Handle components carefully | Rough handling can break connections |"),
  q(7, 'What happens when a short circuit occurs?',
    ['A very large current flows, causing overheating and potential fire', 'The circuit works normally', 'The lamp gets brighter safely', 'The battery lasts longer'], 0,
    'A short circuit bypasses the load, creating a path of very low resistance. Ohm\'s law tells us this means a very large current flows, causing wires to overheat and potentially causing a fire.'),
  t(8, "## Energy Sources for Circuits\n\nElectrical circuits need an energy source. In South Africa, we use many different sources of electrical energy.\n\n### Energy for Heating, Lighting, and Cooking\n\n| Source | Used For | Where in SA |\n|--------|---------|------------|\n| **Coal-fired power stations** | Most of SA's electricity (~80%) | Mpumalanga (Eskom stations) |\n| **Hydroelectric** | Electricity from falling water | Gariep Dam, Drakensberg pump scheme |\n| **Nuclear** | Electricity from nuclear reactions | Koeberg near Cape Town |\n| **Solar** | Electricity from sunlight | Northern Cape solar farms |\n| **Wind** | Electricity from wind turbines | Eastern Cape, Western Cape coast |\n| **Gas/paraffin** | Heating and cooking in homes without electricity | Rural and informal areas |\n| **Wood/coal** | Heating and cooking | Many rural households |\n\n### Ethical Issues: Energy in South Africa\n\n- Many South Africans in rural areas and informal settlements still lack electricity\n- Some people make **illegal connections** to the grid, which are dangerous (electrocution risk) and cause power losses\n- Schools and clinics need reliable power for lighting, computers, and refrigerating medicines\n- The move to **renewable energy** (solar and wind) is creating new jobs but also raising concerns about the future of coal-mining communities\n- **Load shedding** has highlighted the need for alternative energy sources"),
  fb(9, 'South Africa generates about ___% of its electricity from coal. The country\'s only nuclear power station is ___, located near Cape Town.',
    ['80', 'Koeberg'],
    'Coal provides approximately 80% of South Africa\'s electricity. Koeberg Nuclear Power Station near Cape Town is the only nuclear plant in Africa.'),
  q(10, 'Why are illegal electricity connections dangerous?',
    ['They risk electrocution because they bypass safety devices and use incorrect wiring', 'They are too expensive', 'They only work during the day', 'They make lights too bright'], 0,
    'Illegal connections bypass safety devices (circuit breakers, proper insulation) and use incorrect wiring. This creates a serious risk of electrocution, fire, and power surges.'),
];

// Lesson 5.2: Series and Parallel Circuits, and Logic Gates
blockNum = 0;
const ch5_lesson2 = [
  t(1, "## Series Circuits\n\nIn a **series circuit**, all components are connected in a single path. Current flows through each component one after the other.\n\n### Properties of Series Circuits\n\n| Property | Series Circuit |\n|----------|---------------|\n| **Current** | The same everywhere — all components share the same current |\n| **Voltage** | Shared between components — each gets a fraction of the total |\n| **One path** | There is only one path for current to flow |\n| **If one component fails** | The entire circuit stops working |\n\n### Lamps in Series\n\n- All lamps share the same current\n- Each lamp gets only part of the total voltage, so lamps are **dimmer**\n- Adding more lamps makes each one even dimmer\n- If one lamp breaks, all lamps go out (circuit is broken)\n\n### Cells in Series\n\n- Cells must all face the same direction (positive to negative)\n- **Voltages add up:** 3 cells × 1.5 V = 4.5 V\n- Same current capacity as a single cell (battery life unchanged)"),
  t(2, "## Parallel Circuits\n\nIn a **parallel circuit**, components are connected on separate branches. Current splits and flows through each branch independently.\n\n### Properties of Parallel Circuits\n\n| Property | Parallel Circuit |\n|----------|------------------|\n| **Current** | Splits between branches — more branches = more total current from battery |\n| **Voltage** | Same across all branches — each component gets the full battery voltage |\n| **Multiple paths** | Current has more than one path to follow |\n| **If one component fails** | Other branches continue working |\n\n### Lamps in Parallel\n\n- Each lamp gets the full battery voltage, so all lamps burn at **full brightness**\n- If one lamp breaks, the others stay on\n- Adding more lamps draws more current from the battery (battery drains faster)\n\n### Cells in Parallel\n\n- Cells are connected positive-to-positive and negative-to-negative\n- Voltage stays the same as one cell (1.5 V)\n- **Battery lasts longer** because the current demand is shared between cells\n\n### South African Household Wiring\n\nHouses in South Africa are wired in **parallel**. This means:\n- Each appliance gets the full 230 V\n- If one light blows, the others stay on\n- Each circuit has its own **circuit breaker** for safety"),
  q(3, 'What happens to the other lamps in a parallel circuit when one lamp blows?',
    ['They stay on because each has its own path to the battery', 'They all go out', 'They get dimmer', 'They get brighter'], 0,
    'In a parallel circuit, each lamp has its own independent path. If one lamp fails, current continues to flow through the other branches, so the other lamps stay on.'),
  t(4, "## Comparing Series and Parallel Circuits\n\n| Feature | Series | Parallel |\n|---------|--------|----------|\n| **Paths for current** | One | Multiple |\n| **Current** | Same through all components | Splits between branches |\n| **Voltage** | Shared between components | Same across all branches |\n| **Lamp brightness** | Dimmer (shared voltage) | Full brightness |\n| **If one fails** | All stop | Others continue |\n| **Battery life** | Longer (less total current) | Shorter (more total current) |\n| **Use** | Christmas lights (old style), simple switches | Home wiring, car electrical systems |\n\n### Why Parallel Is Used in Homes\n\n1. Each appliance gets the full voltage — it works at its designed power\n2. Appliances can be switched on and off independently\n3. If one appliance breaks, it does not affect others\n4. Each circuit can have its own fuse or circuit breaker for safety\n\n### Drawing Series vs Parallel Circuits\n\n- **Series:** Components are in a line, one after another, forming a single loop\n- **Parallel:** The main wire splits into branches, each branch has its own component, and branches rejoin before returning to the battery"),
  fb(5, 'In a series circuit, the ___ is the same through all components. In a parallel circuit, the ___ is the same across all branches.',
    ['current', 'voltage'],
    'Series circuits have the same current everywhere (one path). Parallel circuits have the same voltage across all branches (each branch connects directly to the battery terminals).'),
  t(6, "## Switches in Series and Parallel — Logic Gates\n\n### Switches in Series = AND Logic\n\nWhen two switches are in **series**, **both** must be closed for the circuit to work.\n\n| Switch A | Switch B | Lamp |\n|----------|----------|------|\n| Open | Open | OFF |\n| Open | Closed | OFF |\n| Closed | Open | OFF |\n| Closed | Closed | **ON** |\n\nThis is called an **AND gate** — Switch A **AND** Switch B must be closed.\n\n**Application:** Safety systems where two buttons must be pressed at the same time (e.g. a cutting machine requires both hands on buttons).\n\n### Switches in Parallel = OR Logic\n\nWhen two switches are in **parallel**, **either** can be closed for the circuit to work.\n\n| Switch A | Switch B | Lamp |\n|----------|----------|------|\n| Open | Open | OFF |\n| Open | Closed | **ON** |\n| Closed | Open | **ON** |\n| Closed | Closed | **ON** |\n\nThis is called an **OR gate** — Switch A **OR** Switch B must be closed.\n\n**Application:** A hallway light with two switches — one at each end of the hallway."),
  q(7, 'Two switches are connected in series. Switch A is closed but Switch B is open. What is the state of the lamp?',
    ['OFF — both switches must be closed in a series (AND) circuit', 'ON — one switch is enough', 'Flickering', 'Dim'], 0,
    'In a series circuit (AND logic), ALL switches must be closed for current to flow. If any switch is open, the circuit is broken and the lamp stays off.'),
  t(8, "## Truth Tables\n\nA **truth table** is a table that shows all possible combinations of switch positions and the resulting output.\n\n### AND Truth Table (Series Switches)\n\n| Input A | Input B | Output |\n|---------|---------|--------|\n| 0 (Open) | 0 (Open) | 0 (OFF) |\n| 0 (Open) | 1 (Closed) | 0 (OFF) |\n| 1 (Closed) | 0 (Open) | 0 (OFF) |\n| 1 (Closed) | 1 (Closed) | 1 (ON) |\n\n### OR Truth Table (Parallel Switches)\n\n| Input A | Input B | Output |\n|---------|---------|--------|\n| 0 (Open) | 0 (Open) | 0 (OFF) |\n| 0 (Open) | 1 (Closed) | 1 (ON) |\n| 1 (Closed) | 0 (Open) | 1 (ON) |\n| 1 (Closed) | 1 (Closed) | 1 (ON) |\n\n### Conventions\n\n- **1** means ON, closed, or TRUE\n- **0** means OFF, open, or FALSE\n\n### Writing Truth Tables\n\n1. List all possible input combinations (for 2 inputs, there are 4 combinations)\n2. For each combination, determine whether the output is ON or OFF\n3. Present the results neatly in a table\n\nTruth tables are the foundation of digital electronics and computer logic. Every computer processor uses millions of logic gates to process information."),
  fb(9, 'In a truth table, 1 represents ___ (closed) and 0 represents ___ (open). The AND gate only produces output 1 when ___ inputs are 1.',
    ['ON', 'OFF', 'both'],
    'In digital logic: 1 = ON/closed/TRUE and 0 = OFF/open/FALSE. An AND gate requires all inputs to be 1 to produce an output of 1.'),
  q(10, 'A security system requires that EITHER a key card OR a fingerprint scan must be used to open the door. Which logic gate is this?',
    ['OR gate', 'AND gate', 'NOT gate', 'XOR gate'], 0,
    'An OR gate produces output when at LEAST ONE input is active. Since either the key card or the fingerprint scan is enough to open the door, this is OR logic.'),
];

