// Lesson 6.1: Generating Electricity and Renewable Energy
blockNum = 0;
const ch6_lesson1 = [
  t(1, "## How South Africa Generates Electricity\n\nSouth Africa uses several methods to generate electricity. Understanding these sources is important for evaluating the **impact of technology** on our country.\n\n### Thermal Power Stations (Coal)\n\nEskom generates about **80%** of South Africa's electricity using coal.\n\n**How it works:**\n1. Coal is burned in a boiler to heat water\n2. Water turns to **steam** at high pressure\n3. Steam spins a **turbine** (a set of fan-like blades)\n4. The turbine turns a **generator** (which converts rotary motion into electricity)\n5. Electricity is sent to the **national grid** through high-voltage transmission lines\n6. **Step-up transformers** increase the voltage for long-distance transmission\n7. **Step-down transformers** decrease the voltage for homes and businesses\n\n### Systems Diagram for a Coal Power Station\n\n| Input | Process | Output |\n|-------|---------|--------|\n| Chemical energy in coal | Burn → Steam → Turbine → Generator | Electrical energy to the grid |\n\n### Advantages and Disadvantages\n\n| Advantages | Disadvantages |\n|-----------|---------------|\n| Reliable — works 24/7 | Burns fossil fuel — produces CO₂ and air pollution |\n| SA has large coal reserves | Coal is non-renewable — will eventually run out |\n| Creates many jobs in mining | Causes acid mine drainage and land degradation |"),
  t(2, "## Renewable Energy Sources\n\n**Renewable energy** comes from sources that are naturally replenished and will not run out.\n\n### Solar Energy\n\n| Feature | Detail |\n|---------|--------|\n| **How it works** | Photovoltaic (PV) cells convert sunlight directly into electricity |\n| **Where in SA** | Northern Cape (e.g. the Jasper Solar Farm near Kimberley) |\n| **Advantages** | Clean, free fuel, no pollution, low maintenance |\n| **Disadvantages** | Only works during daytime, expensive to set up, needs large areas |\n\n### Wind Energy\n\n| Feature | Detail |\n|---------|--------|\n| **How it works** | Wind turns large turbine blades, which spin a generator |\n| **Where in SA** | Eastern Cape coast, Western Cape coast |\n| **Advantages** | Clean, free fuel, no pollution |\n| **Disadvantages** | Wind is unpredictable, turbines are noisy, affect bird life |\n\n### Hydroelectric Energy\n\n| Feature | Detail |\n|---------|--------|\n| **How it works** | Falling water spins a turbine connected to a generator |\n| **Where in SA** | Gariep Dam on Orange River, Drakensberg Pumped Storage Scheme |\n| **Advantages** | Clean, reliable, long lifespan |\n| **Disadvantages** | Requires a suitable river/dam site, can disrupt ecosystems |"),
  q(3, 'In a coal-fired power station, what device converts the rotary motion of the turbine into electrical energy?',
    ['Generator', 'Transformer', 'Motor', 'Boiler'], 0,
    'A generator converts mechanical (rotary) energy into electrical energy. The turbine spins the generator, which produces electricity through electromagnetic induction.'),
  t(4, "## Nuclear Energy and the National Grid\n\n### Nuclear Power\n\nSouth Africa has one nuclear power station — **Koeberg**, located near Cape Town.\n\n| Feature | Detail |\n|---------|--------|\n| **How it works** | Nuclear fission (splitting uranium atoms) releases heat, which makes steam, which spins a turbine |\n| **Advantages** | Produces no CO₂ during operation, reliable, produces large amounts of electricity |\n| **Disadvantages** | Radioactive waste is dangerous for thousands of years, risk of nuclear accidents, expensive to build |\n\n### The National Grid\n\nThe **national grid** is the network of power lines and transformers that distributes electricity across South Africa.\n\n| Stage | What Happens |\n|-------|-------------|\n| **Generation** | Power station generates electricity at ~11 000 V |\n| **Step-up transformer** | Increases voltage to 132 000–765 000 V for long-distance transmission |\n| **Transmission lines** | High-voltage lines carry electricity across the country (Eskom pylons) |\n| **Step-down transformer** | Reduces voltage to 230 V for homes or 400 V for factories |\n| **Distribution** | Electricity reaches homes, schools, and businesses |\n\n### Alternating Current (AC) vs Direct Current (DC)\n\n| Feature | AC | DC |\n|---------|-------|-------|\n| **Direction** | Current changes direction 50 times per second (50 Hz) | Current flows in one direction only |\n| **Source** | Generators, mains electricity | Batteries, solar cells |\n| **Use** | Power transmission, appliances | Electronics, torches, cellphones |"),
  fb(5, 'Transformers that increase voltage for long-distance transmission are called ___-up transformers. South African mains electricity is ___ current at 230 V.',
    ['step', 'alternating'],
    'Step-up transformers increase voltage (reducing current) for efficient long-distance transmission. South African homes receive 230 V AC (alternating current) at 50 Hz.'),
  t(6, "## Electrochemical Cells — Batteries\n\n### How a Cell Works\n\nA **cell** converts **chemical energy** into **electrical energy** through a chemical reaction.\n\n### Parts of a Simple Cell\n\n| Part | Material | Function |\n|------|---------|----------|\n| **Positive electrode** | Copper or carbon rod | Collects electrons |\n| **Negative electrode** | Zinc | Releases electrons |\n| **Electrolyte** | Acid solution or paste | Allows ions to flow between electrodes |\n\n### Types of Cells\n\n| Type | Recharges? | Examples |\n|------|-----------|----------|\n| **Primary cell** | No — once flat, it is discarded | Zinc-carbon cells (AA, D), alkaline cells |\n| **Secondary cell** | Yes — can be recharged many times | Lead-acid (car battery), lithium-ion (cellphone) |\n\n### Advantages and Disadvantages of Batteries\n\n| Advantages | Disadvantages |\n|-----------|---------------|\n| Portable — no wires needed | Limited lifespan — run out or degrade |\n| Provide DC for electronics | Contain toxic chemicals (lead, acid, lithium) |\n| Available everywhere | Must be disposed of properly (e-waste) |\n\n### Practical: Making Your Own Cell\n\nYou can make a simple cell using:\n- A **zinc strip** (negative electrode)\n- A **copper strip** (positive electrode)\n- **Lemon juice or vinegar** (electrolyte)\n- Connect a voltmeter to measure the voltage produced (typically 0.5–1.0 V)"),
  q(7, 'Which type of cell can be recharged and used again?',
    ['Secondary cell', 'Primary cell', 'Fuel cell', 'Solar cell'], 0,
    'Secondary cells (like car batteries and cellphone batteries) can be recharged many times through a reversible chemical reaction. Primary cells cannot be recharged.'),
  t(8, "## Advantages and Disadvantages of Series and Parallel Battery Connections\n\n### Cells in Series\n\n- Connect positive terminal to negative terminal of the next cell\n- **Voltages add up:** 3 × 1.5 V = 4.5 V\n- Battery life is the same as one cell\n- Used when higher voltage is needed (e.g. a torch with 3 cells = 4.5 V)\n\n### Cells in Parallel\n\n- Connect all positive terminals together and all negative terminals together\n- **Voltage stays the same** as one cell (1.5 V)\n- **Battery life increases** because the current demand is shared\n- Used when longer battery life is needed\n\n### Practical Investigation: Ohm's Law Introduction\n\nConnect one cell, then two cells, then three cells in series to a small lamp. Observe:\n\n| Number of Cells | Voltage | Brightness |\n|----------------|---------|------------|\n| 1 | 1.5 V | Dim |\n| 2 | 3.0 V | Medium |\n| 3 | 4.5 V | Bright |\n\n**Conclusion:** As voltage increases, more current flows and the lamp gets brighter. This is the beginning of **Ohm's Law** — the relationship between voltage, current, and resistance, which you will study in more detail in Grade 9.\n\n$$V = I \\times R$$\n\nWhere V = voltage (volts), I = current (amperes), R = resistance (ohms)."),
  fb(9, 'Cells in series produce a ___ voltage. Cells in parallel produce the same voltage but with ___ battery life.',
    ['higher', 'longer'],
    'Series cells add their voltages together (higher total voltage). Parallel cells share the current load, so each cell works less hard, lasting longer.'),
  q(10, 'A torch uses 3 cells of 1.5 V each connected in series. What is the total voltage?',
    ['4.5 V', '1.5 V', '3.0 V', '6.0 V'], 0,
    'Cells in series: voltages add up. 3 × 1.5 V = 4.5 V total.'),
];

