// Lesson 7.1: Drawing Circuits, Design Brief for Electrical Projects
blockNum = 0;
const ch7_lesson1 = [
  t(1, "## Drawing Electrical Circuit Diagrams\n\nCircuit diagrams are a form of **graphic communication** used in Technology. They use standard symbols so that anyone can understand the circuit.\n\n### Rules for Drawing Circuit Diagrams\n\n1. Use a **ruler** — all wires must be drawn as straight horizontal or vertical lines\n2. Use the **correct symbol** for each component\n3. Start and end at the battery\n4. **Label** components with their values where needed\n5. Show the **direction of conventional current** (from + to − of the battery)\n6. Make the diagram **neat and clear** — avoid crossing wires unless necessary\n7. Place the battery at the top of the diagram\n\n### Step-by-Step: Drawing a Simple Circuit\n\n1. Draw the battery symbol at the top (long line = positive, short line = negative)\n2. Draw a line from the positive terminal to the right\n3. Add a switch symbol along this line\n4. Continue the line down to the load (lamp, buzzer, or motor)\n5. Draw a line from the load back to the negative terminal of the battery\n6. Check: Is the circuit a complete loop?\n\n### Common Mistakes to Avoid\n\n- Forgetting to close the circuit (leaving a gap)\n- Drawing diagonal lines instead of horizontal/vertical\n- Using wrong symbols\n- Not labelling components\n- Drawing a short circuit (wire from + to − with no load)"),
  t(2, "## Designing an Electrical System: IDMEC for Circuits\n\nThe **IDMEC** design process applies to electrical projects just as it does to structures.\n\n### Design Brief for an Electrical Project\n\nA design brief for an electrical project must include:\n1. **What** you will design and make (e.g. a panic button system, a doorbell)\n2. **Specifications** — what the circuit must do:\n   - Type of energy source (battery voltage)\n   - Type of output (light, sound, motion)\n   - Any logic requirements (AND or OR switching)\n3. **Constraints** — limitations:\n   - Budget (cost of components)\n   - Available components\n   - Time to complete\n\n### Example Design Brief\n\n> Design and make a model of a **panic button alarm system** for a home security scenario. The system must:\n> - Use a 4.5 V battery (3 cells in series)\n> - Have a push-button switch that activates a buzzer AND an LED\n> - Include a series resistor to protect the LED\n> - Fit inside a cardboard housing no larger than 10 cm × 8 cm × 5 cm\n> - Cost no more than R60 in components\n\n### Communicating the Circuit Design\n\n1. Draw the circuit diagram using correct symbols and a ruler\n2. Draw a **systems diagram** (Input → Process → Output)\n3. Draw a **3D sketch** of the housing/enclosure\n4. Create a **bill of materials** listing each component and its cost"),
  q(3, 'In a circuit diagram, what does a long thin line next to a short thick line represent?',
    ['A cell (battery)', 'A switch', 'A resistor', 'A lamp'], 0,
    'The cell symbol has one long thin line (positive terminal) and one short thick line (negative terminal). A battery symbol shows multiple cells connected in series.'),
  t(4, "## Practical: Drawing Series and Parallel Circuits with Resistors\n\n### Circuit 1: Resistors in Series\n\nWhen resistors are connected in **series**, their resistances **add up**:\n\n$$R_{total} = R_1 + R_2 + R_3$$\n\n**Example:** Three resistors of 100 Ω, 200 Ω, and 300 Ω in series:\n$$R_{total} = 100 + 200 + 300 = 600 \\text{ Ω}$$\n\n### Circuit 2: Resistors in Parallel\n\nWhen resistors are connected in **parallel**, the total resistance is **less than the smallest individual resistor**:\n\n$$\\frac{1}{R_{total}} = \\frac{1}{R_1} + \\frac{1}{R_2}$$\n\n**Example:** Two resistors of 100 Ω each in parallel:\n$$\\frac{1}{R_{total}} = \\frac{1}{100} + \\frac{1}{100} = \\frac{2}{100}$$\n$$R_{total} = \\frac{100}{2} = 50 \\text{ Ω}$$\n\n### Key Insight\n\n- **Series:** Total resistance increases (harder for current to flow)\n- **Parallel:** Total resistance decreases (easier for current to flow)\n\nThis is why adding more lamps in parallel makes the battery drain faster — the total resistance drops, so more current flows."),
  fb(5, 'Two resistors of 200 Ω and 300 Ω connected in series have a total resistance of ___ Ω. Resistors in parallel always have a total resistance ___ than the smallest individual resistor.',
    ['500', 'less'],
    'In series: R_total = 200 + 300 = 500 Ω. In parallel, total resistance is always less than the smallest resistor because current has more paths to flow through.'),
  t(6, "## Practical Circuit: Connect Resistors in Series and Parallel\n\n### Practical Activity\n\n**Aim:** To observe the effect of connecting resistors in series and parallel on the brightness of a lamp.\n\n### Equipment\n\n- 3 × 1.5 V cells, cell holder\n- 2 × identical lamps and lamp holders\n- 3 × identical resistors (e.g. 100 Ω)\n- Switch, connecting wires\n\n### Method\n\n**Experiment 1: Resistors in Series**\n1. Connect one cell, switch, and one resistor in series with a lamp\n2. Note the brightness of the lamp\n3. Add a second resistor in series\n4. Observe — the lamp gets **dimmer** (more resistance = less current)\n5. Add a third resistor — lamp gets even dimmer\n\n**Experiment 2: Resistors in Parallel**\n1. Connect one cell, switch, and one resistor in series with a lamp\n2. Note the brightness\n3. Connect a second resistor in **parallel** with the first\n4. Observe — the lamp gets **brighter** (less total resistance = more current)\n\n### Results Table\n\n| Configuration | Total Resistance | Lamp Brightness |\n|--------------|-----------------|----------------|\n| 1 resistor | 100 Ω | Medium |\n| 2 resistors in series | 200 Ω | Dim |\n| 3 resistors in series | 300 Ω | Very dim |\n| 2 resistors in parallel | 50 Ω | Bright |"),
  q(7, 'When you add more resistors in series to a circuit, what happens to the total resistance?',
    ['It increases', 'It decreases', 'It stays the same', 'It becomes zero'], 0,
    'In series, resistances add up: R_total = R1 + R2 + R3 + ... Adding more resistors increases total resistance, which reduces current and makes the lamp dimmer.'),
  t(8, "## End-of-Year Consolidation: Electrical Systems\n\n### Key Concepts Summary\n\n| Concept | Key Facts |\n|---------|----------|\n| **Circuit requirements** | Energy source + conductor + load + complete path |\n| **Series circuit** | One path, same current, voltage shared |\n| **Parallel circuit** | Multiple paths, same voltage, current shared |\n| **AND logic** | Switches in series — all must be closed |\n| **OR logic** | Switches in parallel — at least one must be closed |\n| **Cells in series** | Voltages add up |\n| **Cells in parallel** | Voltage same, battery lasts longer |\n| **Resistors in series** | Resistances add up |\n| **Resistors in parallel** | Total resistance decreases |\n| **Short circuit** | Dangerous — wire bypasses load, large current flows |\n\n### Safety Reminders\n\n- Only use batteries (low voltage DC) in school projects\n- South African mains electricity is **230 V AC** — lethal, only for qualified electricians\n- Always check circuits before switching on\n- Never create short circuits\n- Dispose of batteries responsibly — they contain toxic chemicals"),
  fb(9, 'Switches in series follow ___ logic. Switches in parallel follow ___ logic. South African mains electricity is ___ V.',
    ['AND', 'OR', '230'],
    'Series switches = AND (all must be closed). Parallel switches = OR (at least one must be closed). SA mains = 230 V AC.'),
  q(10, 'Why are homes wired in parallel rather than series?',
    ['Each appliance gets the full voltage and can be switched independently', 'It is cheaper', 'Series circuits are illegal', 'Parallel uses less wire'], 0,
    'Parallel wiring ensures each appliance receives the full mains voltage (230 V), works at its designed power, and can be switched on/off independently. If one appliance fails, the others continue working.'),
];

