blockNum = 0;
const ch8_lesson1 = [
  t(1, "## Grade 8 Technology — Comprehensive Revision\n\n### Mechanical Systems and Control\n\n**Mechanisms** change input force/motion into useful output force/motion.\n\n**Levers:**\n- Class 1: Effort — Fulcrum — Load (seesaw, crowbar)\n- Class 2: Effort — Load — Fulcrum (wheelbarrow, nutcracker)\n- Class 3: Load — Effort — Fulcrum (fishing rod, tweezers)\n\n**Mechanical Advantage:** MA = Load ÷ Effort\n- MA > 1: force is multiplied\n- MA < 1: speed/distance is multiplied\n- Trade-off: gain in force = loss in distance\n\n**Linkages:** Reverse motion, push-pull, parallel motion, bell crank (90°), crank and slider.\n\n**Motion types:** Rotary (circular), reciprocating (back-and-forth in a line), oscillating (swinging), linear (straight line one direction)."),
  t(2, "### Gear Systems\n\n**Gear ratio** = Teeth on driven ÷ Teeth on driver\n- Large driven gear → slower speed, more torque\n- Small driven gear → faster speed, less torque\n- Idler gear reverses direction without changing ratio\n\n**Gear types:**\n- Spur gears: parallel shafts, counter-rotate\n- Bevel gears: axes at 90°\n- Worm gear: large speed reduction, self-locking\n- Rack and pinion: rotary → linear motion\n\n**Compound gear trains:** Total ratio = product of stage ratios\n\n### Structures\n\n**Types:** Frame (skeleton), Shell (hollow skin), Solid (mass), Combined.\n\n**Forces:** Tension (pull), Compression (push), Bending, Torsion (twist), Shear (cut).\n\n**Structural members:** Struts (compression), Ties (tension), Beams, Columns.\n\n**Strengthening:** Triangulation (strongest shape = triangle), Cross-bracing, Corrugation.\n\n**Failure:** Fracture (breaks), Bending/buckling (deforms), Toppling (falls over — centre of gravity outside base)."),
  q(3, 'What is the gear ratio when a driver gear has 12 teeth and a driven gear has 48 teeth?',
    ['4:1', '1:4', '12:1', '48:1'], 0,
    'Gear ratio = driven ÷ driver = 48 ÷ 12 = 4:1. The driven gear turns 4 times slower with 4 times more torque.'),
  t(4, "### Processing and Impact of Technology\n\n**Processing:** Changing raw materials into useful products.\n\n**Packaging functions:** Protection, Preservation, Information, Marketing, Containment, Convenience.\n\n**Impact of technology:**\n- Positive: Jobs, health, communication, education, transport\n- Negative: Pollution, job losses, inequality, waste, resource depletion\n\n**Bias in technology:** Gender, economic, cultural, geographic, access.\n\n**Indigenous Knowledge Systems (IKS):** Thatched roofs, clay grain storage, iron smelting (200 AD), herbal medicine.\n\n**3 Rs:** Reduce (first priority), Reuse, Recycle.\n\n**Material properties:** Strength, hardness, stiffness, flexibility, elasticity, brittleness, toughness, ductility, malleability.\n\n**Adapting materials:** Reinforced concrete, plywood, laminating, corrugation.\n\n**Metal sections:** I-beam (most efficient for bending), T, L, channel, tube."),
  fb(5, 'The three types of structural failure are fracture, bending, and ___. The most efficient metal beam shape for resisting bending is the ___.',
    ['toppling', 'I-beam'],
    'Fracture (breaking), bending (deforming), and toppling (falling over) are the three failure modes. The I-beam concentrates material at the flanges where bending forces are greatest.'),
  t(6, "### Electrical Systems\n\n**Circuit requirements:** Energy source, conductor, load, complete path.\n\n**Series circuit:** One path, same current everywhere, voltage shared, if one fails all fail.\n**Parallel circuit:** Multiple paths, same voltage everywhere, current shared, if one fails others continue.\n\n**AND logic:** Switches in series — all must be closed.\n**OR logic:** Switches in parallel — at least one must be closed.\n\n**Truth tables:** Show all input combinations and resulting outputs.\n\n**Cells in series:** Voltages add up (3 × 1.5 V = 4.5 V).\n**Cells in parallel:** Same voltage, longer battery life.\n\n**Resistors in series:** R_total = R1 + R2 + R3 (total increases).\n**Resistors in parallel:** Total resistance decreases (less than smallest).\n\n**Energy sources:** Coal (80% of SA electricity), nuclear (Koeberg), solar, wind, hydroelectric.\n\n**AC vs DC:** Mains = 230 V AC. Batteries = DC.\n\n**Safety:** Only use low-voltage DC in school. Never work with mains electricity. Never create short circuits."),
  q(7, 'Three resistors of 100 Ω each are connected in series. What is the total resistance?',
    ['300 Ω', '100 Ω', '33.3 Ω', '900 Ω'], 0,
    'Resistors in series: R_total = R1 + R2 + R3 = 100 + 100 + 100 = 300 Ω.'),
  t(8, "### The IDMEC Design Process — Revision\n\n| Step | Key Activities |\n|------|---------------|\n| **Investigate** | Research the problem, analyse existing products, gather information |\n| **Design** | Design brief (specifications + constraints), sketch 2+ ideas, evaluate and choose best |\n| **Make** | Flow chart, list tools/materials, build model safely |\n| **Evaluate** | Test against specifications, identify strengths/weaknesses, suggest improvements |\n| **Communicate** | Present with drawings, reports, models, budget |\n\n### Graphic Communication\n\n**Line types:**\n- Continuous thick: visible outlines\n- Continuous thin: dimensions, projections\n- Dashed: hidden detail\n- Chain: centre lines, symmetry axes\n\n**Dimensioning:** Thin lines with arrowheads, dimensions in mm, outside the object where possible.\n\n### South African Context Review\n\n- Eskom pylons: triangulated frame structures, galvanised steel\n- Mining: gear systems, environmental impact, IKS (iron smelting)\n- Energy: 80% coal, Koeberg nuclear, growing solar/wind\n- Materials: corrugated iron roofing, reinforced concrete, recycling\n- Bias: Digital divide, gender gap in STEM, urban vs rural access"),
  fb(9, 'The five steps of IDMEC are Investigate, Design, ___, Evaluate, and Communicate. Dimensions on technical drawings are always given in ___.',
    ['Make', 'millimetres'],
    'IDMEC: Investigate, Design, Make, Evaluate, Communicate. All dimensions on South African technical drawings use millimetres (mm) as the standard unit.'),
  q(10, 'Why is the triangle considered the strongest shape in structural engineering?',
    ['It cannot change shape without breaking one of its members', 'It has three sides', 'It is easy to draw', 'It uses the least material'], 0,
    'A triangle is rigid — its shape is completely determined by the length of its three sides. A rectangle can deform into a parallelogram, but a triangle cannot deform without a member breaking.'),
  t(11, "### Key Formulas\n\n| Formula | Use |\n|---------|----|\n| MA = Load ÷ Effort | Mechanical advantage of any machine |\n| MA = Effort distance ÷ Load distance | Mechanical advantage of a lever |\n| Gear Ratio = Driven teeth ÷ Driver teeth | Speed and force change in gears |\n| Speed of driven = Speed of driver ÷ Gear ratio | Output speed of a gear system |\n| Compound ratio = Stage 1 ratio × Stage 2 ratio | Total ratio of a compound gear train |\n| V = I × R | Ohm's Law (introduction) |\n| R_series = R1 + R2 + R3 | Resistors in series |\n\n### Final Checklist\n\n- Can you identify and classify levers (class 1, 2, 3)?\n- Can you calculate gear ratios and output speeds?\n- Can you name and describe the five forces (tension, compression, bending, torsion, shear)?\n- Can you draw a circuit diagram with correct symbols?\n- Can you complete AND and OR truth tables?\n- Can you discuss the impact of technology (positive and negative)?\n- Can you give examples of indigenous knowledge systems?\n- Can you follow the IDMEC design process?"),
  q(12, 'A compound gear train has stage 1 ratio of 3:1 and stage 2 ratio of 5:1. What is the total gear ratio?',
    ['15:1', '8:1', '2:1', '35:1'], 0,
    'Total ratio = stage 1 × stage 2 = 3 × 5 = 15:1. The output turns 15 times slower than the input, with 15 times more torque.'),
];

